course: world
lesson: 47
title: Natural resources and why we must save them
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Natural resources are the things people take from nature to live: water, air, soil, wood, fish, sunlight, wind, and things dug from the ground such as coal, oil, gas, iron ore and salt.
explain: Some resources renew themselves: sunlight and wind come every day, and a forest can grow back if people plant trees and cut no more than grows.
explain: Others do not renew in a human lifetime: coal, oil and gas formed from plants and tiny sea creatures buried for millions of years, so once we burn them they are gone for good.
explain: Burning coal, oil and gas also puts smoke and carbon dioxide into the air, which dirties the air we breathe and warms the climate of the whole planet.
explain: So we save resources: we turn off lights and taps, sort rubbish so that paper, glass, metal and plastic can be recycled, use things again, and get energy from the Sun and wind; one tonne of recycled paper saves many trees from being cut.
fact: Natural resources are materials and energy that people take from nature.
fact: Renewable resources, such as sunlight, wind and flowing water, are not used up.
fact: Forests, fish and soil renew themselves only if people do not use them faster than they grow back.
fact: Rich farm soil, like that of the American Great Plains, takes thousands of years to form, and wind and rain can carry it away quickly from bare fields.
fact: Coal, oil and natural gas are fossil fuels formed over millions of years from ancient plants and animals.
fact: Fossil fuels are non-renewable: once burned they cannot be made again in a human lifetime.
fact: Burning fossil fuels pollutes the air and adds carbon dioxide, which warms the Earth's climate.
fact: Australia digs up huge amounts of iron ore and coal, and Canada has great forests and much oil in Alberta.
fact: Recycling means making new things from used materials such as paper, glass, metal and plastic.
fact: Only a tiny part of all the water on Earth is fresh water that people can drink.
fact: Solar panels make electricity from sunlight and wind turbines make it from the wind.
fact: Reduce, reuse, recycle: use less, use things again, and turn waste into new materials.
exercise: What are natural resources? | Things people take from nature to live | Things made in factories from plastic | Only the gold and diamonds in the ground | Only the food sold in shops
exercise: Which of these is a renewable resource? | Sunlight | Coal | Oil | Natural gas
exercise: Which of these is a non-renewable resource? | Oil | Wind | Sunlight | Flowing river water
exercise: How were coal, oil and gas formed? | From ancient plants and animals buried for millions of years | From rocks melted in volcanoes last century | From rain soaking deep into the ground each year | From sand pressed together by the sea
exercise: Why are fossil fuels called non-renewable? | They take millions of years to form | They can be grown again on farms each year | They are made fresh in factories | They come back every spring
exercise: What happens to the air when we burn coal, oil and gas? | It gets smoke and more carbon dioxide | It gets cleaner and fresher to breathe | It gets more oxygen than it had before | Nothing changes in the air at all, ever
exercise: When does a forest stay a renewable resource? | When people cut no more trees than grow back | When people cut all of the trees down at once | When no one ever plants new trees | When the forest is burned every year
exercise: What is recycling? | Making new things from used materials | Throwing all rubbish into one bin | Burning old things to get rid of them | Burying waste deep in the ground
exercise: Which energy source does a solar panel use? | Sunlight | Wind | Coal | Oil
exercise: Which resource does Australia dig up and sell in huge amounts? | Iron ore | Sunlight | Rain water | Wind
exercise: Why must we save fresh water even though the Earth is covered by oceans? | Only a tiny part of the Earth's water is fresh | Ocean water is fresh but far too cold to drink | Fresh water is made only in factories | Rain never falls on land
exercise: What does reduce mean in reduce, reuse, recycle? | Use less of things | Use things again | Make new things from waste | Throw things away sooner
exercise: Which action saves energy at home? | Turning off lights when you leave a room | Leaving the TV on all night while you sleep | Keeping the fridge door open to cool the room | Heating the house with the windows wide open
exercise: Why does recycling paper help forests? | Fewer trees need to be cut for new paper | Paper turns back into trees once it rots in the soil | Recycled paper is made from coal | Trees grow faster near recycling bins
exercise: Why is the rich soil of farmland, like that of the American Great Plains, a precious resource? | It takes thousands of years to form and blows away easily | It burns well and heats houses | It is made fresh every year by rain | It can be melted down into metal
test: A town gets all its electricity from a wind farm and solar panels. Why can it go on doing so for ever? | Wind and sunlight are renewable and are not used up | The town has a huge store of coal hidden deep underground | The panels make new sunlight every day | Wind turbines turn even without wind
test: Jack says: When the coal mines are empty, we will just wait for new coal to form. What is wrong with his idea? | Coal takes millions of years to form | Coal forms again in about one year | Coal is made in factories, not in mines | Nothing is wrong with his idea
test: Which is the best way to keep the fish in a lake for the future? | Catch fewer fish than are born each year | Catch as many fish as possible now while they are there | Never feed the fish anything | Pour fresh water into the lake
test: A family sorts its rubbish into paper, glass, metal and plastic. What does this help to do? | Recycle materials into new things | Burn the rubbish faster in the garden | Make more rubbish to sort next week | Turn the plastic into fresh drinking water
test: Which of these resources would run out if people used it for a very long time? | Natural gas | Sunlight | Wind | Ocean tides
test: Why does burning less coal and oil help the whole planet, not only one city? | It adds less carbon dioxide, which warms the whole climate | It makes the Sun shine more brightly | It makes the oceans less salty | It makes the plates of the Earth move slower
test: Which is an example of reusing? | Filling an old jar with buttons | Throwing the jar in the rubbish | Melting the jar to make a new bottle | Buying a new jar every day
test: Why is a tap left running a waste of a precious resource? | Fresh drinkable water is only a tiny part of all water | Water from taps comes straight from the ocean and never ends | Running water makes the pipes grow | The water turns into gas in the sink
test: Which pair is correct? | Australia and iron ore | The Sahara and great forests | Antarctica and wheat fields | The Pacific Ocean and coal mines
test: why cant we just make more oil when it runs out | It took millions of years to form from ancient life | Oil is made in factories very slowly | It can be grown on farms | Oil only forms in winter
test: whats the point of recycling a can tbh | The metal is made into new things instead of digging up more | Cans disappear on their own anyway | It only makes the bin lighter | It makes the can heavier for later
test: my uncle says there's loads of water so we can waste it. true? | No, only a tiny part of Earth's water is fresh water we can drink | Yes, the sea can be drunk as it is | Yes, fresh water never runs out | No, there is no fresh water left at all
test: a video said wind and sunlight will run out soon. right? | No, they are renewable and are not used up | Yes, they will run out like coal in a few years | Yes, sunlight is a fossil fuel | No, but only wind is renewable
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises renewable resources? | They are not exhausted by use, as sunlight and wind | They are formed over millions of years beneath the ground, as coal | They may be used once only, as natural gas | They are obtained solely by mining
exam: Identify, from among the following, a fossil fuel. | Oil | Wind | Sunlight | Flowing water
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the burning of fossil fuels. | It pollutes the air and adds carbon dioxide that warms the climate | It removes carbon dioxide from the air | It renews the supply of fossil fuels | It produces no gases whatsoever
exam: State the proportion of the Earth's water that is fresh water fit for drinking. | Only a very small part | Approximately one half | The greater part | The entirety
